Package: multiCollglm
Type: Package
Title: Collinearity Diagnostics for Generalized Linear Models
Version: 0.4.0
Authors@R: c(
    person(given = "Catalina", family = "Garcia Garcia",
           email = "cbgarcia@go.ugr.es", role = c("aut", "cre")),
    person(given = "Roman", family = "Salmeron Gomez", role = "aut")
    )
Description: Tools to diagnose collinearity in generalized linear models
    (GLMs) via the condition number, a full Belsley-Kuh-Welsch variance-
    decomposition analysis, and the Redefined Variance Inflation Factor
    (RVIF; Salmeron, Garcia and Garcia 2025). By default all three are
    computed, following Weissfeld and Sereika (1991), on the design matrix
    weighted by the IRLS weights at convergence and scaled to unit length
    by column, without centering, so the intercept takes part in the
    diagnosis. The 'method' argument selects among four definitions: the
    Weissfeld and Sereika one ('"WS"', the default), the one of Mackinnon
    and Puterman (1989) ('"MP"'), the one of the original design matrix
    X'X scaled to unit length, without weights ('"X"'), and the
    information matrix X'WX without any transformation ('"RAW"').
    ml_collinearity() implements the diagnostic of Lesaffre and Marx
    (1993), comparing the condition number of the original design matrix
    against Mackinnon and Puterman's (1989) condition number of the
    information matrix to distinguish ordinary collinearity from
    ML-collinearity. Provides methods for 'glm' objects and for
    regularized models fitted with 'glmnet' or 'cv.glmnet', which are
    internally refit as a 'glm' on the active set of variables selected
    at the given lambda. Ships four example datasets (Nitrogen, Mine,
    LeeCancer, Plastic) used by the package's reproduction articles,
    which reproduce and compare against condition numbers reported in the
    published literature.
